03:26:50  [idag] **Article:** "Turbinblad av gummi viker undan när tidvattnet drar för hårt", category Strömningslära (fluid mechanics), id `2026-11-19-gummibladen-som-viker-undan`.
03:26:50  [idag] - **Story:** Zhang and colleagues at the University of Edinburgh, together with Michelin, published "Flexible rubber blades for tidal turbines" in Nature on 11 November 2026 (DOI 10.1038/s41586-026-11278-w). The paper is peer reviewed and open access, and I read the full text including the methods section. In a model turbine and in simulations of a full tidal cycle, the rubber blades gave the same energy as blades twisted by motors. The rapid load swings that wear turbines out dropped by up to one third.
03:26:50  [idag] - **Neighbour check:** the 2026-11-18 article is nuclear physics and 2026-11-17 is astronomy, so there is no clash. The last mechanics article was on 2026-11-10.
03:26:50  [idag] - **Teaser:** the KM3NeT item marked as the newsletter teaser stays frozen until the 2026-11-22 newsletter has gone out, and I didn't touch it.
03:26:50  [idag] - **Images:** no AI image was needed.
03:26:50  [idag]   - The main photo is the TGL DeepGen IV tidal turbine on Orkney, from Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). The credit says the turbine is not part of the study.
03:26:50  [idag]   - Figures 4 and 2 from the paper (CC BY 4.0) are in the article text.
03:26:50  [idag]   - I didn't use Edinburgh's own press photos because they have no stated licence.
03:26:50  [idag] - **Review:** the `utgivare` agent first ruled "fix first". The main points were that the paper has seven authors, not three; the figure 2 caption was wrong; and the deck made a computed result sound like a measurement. I fixed every point. Its follow-up check found that no new review was needed, so the verdict is approved, and both entries are logged in `granskningar.md`.
03:26:50  [idag] - **Glossary:** two new terms, *materialutmattning* (material fatigue) and *bladvinkelreglering* (blade pitch control). I deliberately avoided the word "tidvattenkraft" in the article, because the existing glossary entry with that name means tidal force (gravity's stretching), not tidal power.
03:26:50  [idag] - **Checks:** `verify-begrepp` and `verify-navigation` pass, and so does `verify-sitemap` after running `build-nyheter-og.js`. `verify-sprak` reports three errors, all in older articles.
